{"type":"message","id":"f060b86c","parentId":"e1f92806","timestamp":"2026-03-29T18:41:09.988Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I see the issue - the edit I made earlier created `\\%m` and `\\%d` but the backslash before `%` is invalid in JSON (it's not a recognized escape sequence). The original format `\\%m-\\%d` would work because `\\%` is an escaped backslash, but `\\%m` would be interpreted as backslash + % + m which is also wrong.\n\nWait actually looking at it more carefully:\n- `\\%m` - the backslash escapes the `%` in JSON context, so it means a literal `%` followed by `m`\n- But actually no - `\\%` is not a valid JSON escape, so `\\%m` would cause a JSON parse error\n\n\n- The real solution is to either use a raw string with `%%` or double-escape the backslash to `\\\\%` so the shell receives `%` after the JSON parser processes it\n\n. Looking at the original message format with `$(date +\\%Y-\\%m-\\%d)`, the `\\%` sequence gets interpreted by JSON as an escaped backslash followed by the literal `%m`, which is what we want. So `date +\\%Y-\\%m-\\%d` works because the backslash escaping in JSON is unnecessary and we end up with the literal string `date +%Y-%m-%d` in the command itself.\n\nBut for the new format with `date +\\%Y\\%m\\%d`, the issue is that `\\%` in JSON gets parsed as just `%`, which means we're trying to execute `date +%Y%m%d` - but `%m` and `%d` aren't valid date format specifiers by themselves, they're modifiers that only work when combined with other format characters like `%Y-%m-%d`. The shell would interpret `%m` as a literal string since there's no format specifier before it, which would cause the command to fail or produce unexpected output.","thinkingSignature":"ed4bea31209e711041e9a2d62b1c9691a9621c7527bf9c423c9c8cbbfd7616ae"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_3yxdc8m68160_1","name":"edit","arguments":{"newText":"\"message\": \"备份 openclaw.json：cp ~/.openclaw/openclaw.json ~/.openclaw/ope